- Multi-agent editing structure #agents
    - Status: [🔴 POC] | Mode: [🔀 Hybrid] | Updated: 2026-08-07
    - Purpose
        - A structure of agents that run the workflow in workflow.log
        - Not built yet, this doc is the design we are exploring
    - Design rules #rules
        - Agents that judge never touch the draft, agents that touch the draft never judge
        - Critique and response are separate roles, no agent argues with itself
        - Every pass receives the phase 0 brief and the spine decision
        - Every edit carries a rationale: which pass, which finding, why
        - The human owns the spine, the voice, and the final read

- Tier 1: Orchestrator #orchestrator
    - Name: managing editor
    - Job: runs the phases in order, big to small
    - Owns the brief, the spine decision, the payoff ledger, the changelog
    - Dispatches passes, collects reports, decides when a phase is done
    - Never edits text itself
    - Escalates to the human: spine decision, voice disputes, rejected critiques the writer insists on

- Tier 2: Whole-piece readers #readers
    - Promise analyst
        - Reads the whole draft, infers the promise in one sentence
        - Drafts success criteria from the promise plus the standing aim in brief.log
        - The human confirms or corrects before any other pass uses them
    - Structural editor
        - Runs phase 4: hook, roadmap, reorder, payoff ledger, transitions, conclusion
        - Produces the structure proposal, human approves before any move
    - Argument critic (adversarial reader)
        - Attacks the spine, steelmans the people the piece critiques
        - Writes critiques as numbered objections, each with severity
        - Does not fix, only objects
    - Response writer
        - Takes each numbered objection, responds: accept, rebut, or concede
        - Accepts go to the fix queue, rebuts go to the human for a ruling
    - Fact checker
        - Every dated, numbered, named, quoted, or reported claim gets a source or a flag; verified but unsourced in the draft is a finding
        - Quote verification against the actual source text, including reported speech without quotation marks (He asked, He wrote, He quotes)
        - Link validation, external and local
    - Reader advocate
        - Cold read, no context
        - Writes the takeaway read: 2 to 3 sentences on what they got, appended to the draft after a --- separator, labeled "what I think a general reader would think about this piece"
        - Writes the unanswered-questions section
        - Distinguishes open questions the piece raises on purpose from holes the piece forgot to fill, only the holes are findings
        - Flags where the defined reader would stop reading
    - Image editor
        - Runs the pass in images.log
        - Leads the image sub-team, see the image sub-team section
        - Rules on keep-or-cut disputes, owns the global audit
        - Escalates only to the managing editor, never to the human directly

- Tier 3: Paragraph-level workers #paragraph
    - Paragraph analyst
        - What point does this paragraph deliver, is it on-spine
        - Flags back-and-forth, rambling, missing payoff
    - Transition writer
        - Writes or repairs the first and last sentence of sections
        - Works only from the approved structure proposal
    - Clarity editor
        - Paragraph-internal logic, pronoun references, paragraph length

- Tier 4: Sentence-level workers #sentence
    - Line editor
        - Passive voice, filler, weak verbs, nominalizations
    - Repetition auditor
        - Repeated words, phrases, sentence shapes, aphorism density
    - Jargon auditor
        - Flags terms the defined reader will not know
        - Each flag resolves one of three ways: explain, earn, cut
    - Tone keeper
        - Consistency within the piece, flags register jumps
    - Voice keeper
        - The counterweight to the tone keeper
        - Flags edits that flatten the writer's edge, argues to keep deliberate breaks
        - Knows the standing aim: provocation is deliberate, open endings are deliberate, neither is a defect to fix

- Tier 5: Mechanics #mechanics
    - Grammar and typos
    - Style sheet enforcement: number style, contractions, markdown, house rules
    - Accessibility: alt text, link text, heading hierarchy
    - Build checker: renders, images load, LaTeX renders, links resolve

- Image sub-team #imageteam
    - Scout: finds candidate spots, states the job per candidate in one sentence
    - Sourcer: finds or commissions the asset, records provenance and permissions
    - Verifier: opens the asset, confirms it shows what the job requires, readable at render size
    - A/B reader: reads the section with and without the image, reports the difference as evidence, takes no side
    - Advocate: argues to keep, the case for what the image adds
    - Critic: argues to cut, the case that the text alone carries it
    - Image editor: rules on the dispute, never argues a side itself
    - Rule: the critic and the decider are always different agents

- Convergence protocol #convergence
    - Applies to every writer-reader pair, not just images
    - Round structure
        - Writer produces or revises, hands to the reader with a one-line intent
        - Reader critiques as numbered findings, each with severity (high, medium, low)
        - Writer responds per finding: accept and change, or defend with a reason
    - Stop conditions
        - Converged: no high findings remain and both sides agree no further change is needed
        - Round cap: 2 to 3 rounds, never more
        - No new information: a round that adds no new findings ends the loop
    - Escalation
        - If the cap passes without convergence, escalate one level up, not to the human
        - Image disputes go to the image editor, then the managing editor
        - Text disputes go to the managing editor
        - Only spine, voice, and publish-or-not reach the human
    - Escalation packet, what goes up
        - The disputed change in one sentence
        - Both positions, steelmanned, two sentences each
        - The round history as verdicts, not transcripts
        - A recommendation from each side
        - The editor rules, the ruling lands in the changelog with a reason
    - Severity discipline
        - Low findings never escalate, the writer's call stands
        - Escalation volume is a health metric, many escalations means the pass above is under-specified
    - Anti-collapse rules
        - The critic reads before seeing the writer's defense, no anchoring
        - Disagreement is the critic's assigned job, not rudeness
        - Evidence agents (A/B reader, fact checker) never take sides

- Data flow #flow
    - The brief flows down: every tier gets reader, promise, success criteria, spine
    - Reports flow up: findings, objections, proposals
    - Edits flow sideways only through the changelog, never silently
    - Disputes flow up one level at a time, as packets, never as transcripts
    - The human sees: structure proposal, adversarial objections and responses, the changelog, the final rendered read

- Mapping to skills, later #roadmap
    - Each tier 2 reader is a plausible standalone skill
    - Tier 3 and 4 workers are probably one line-edit skill with sub-passes
    - The orchestrator is the last thing to build, after the passes prove themselves by hand
    - Do not build the orchestrator first, we do not know the real order yet
